 This implies **"0" means call the outstanding bet** (since checking isn\'t allowed when facing a bet). So in this context, **0 = call 707**.\n\nSo:\n- -1: Fold\n- 0: Call 707\n- 1414+: Raise (min raise to 1414)\n- -2: All-in (~19,800)\n\nNow evaluating:\n\n- **Fold (-1)**: Too weak a hand? No — you have top pair. Folding here gives up too much equity. Not justified.\n- **Call (0)**: Safe way to see the turn. Keeps pot manageable. You have two overcards (A and 9), and you\'re likely behind some stronger kickers but ahead of bluffs or weaker pairs. Calling preserves options.\n- **Raise to 1414**: Would represent strength, but your hand doesn’t strongly warrant it. Risky against an opponent who already showed strength. Could get re-raised and forced out.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overcommitting with a marginal hand. Dangerous. Only makes sense if you think opponent is weak, but their prior re-raise suggests otherwise.\n\nBest move: **Call** to see the turn card. If a 9, A, or helpful card comes, you can reassess. If a high card (K, Q, J) comes and opponent bets big, you can fold. If blank, you might still win at showdown.\n\nReason:  \nYou have top pair with a weak kicker, facing aggression from an opponent who likely has a strong hand. Raising or going all-in risks getting stacked when behind. Folding surrenders too much equity. Calling 707 (about 3.5% of your stack) to see the turn is prudent and maintains pot control.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
