\nBut since there’s an Ace and King on board, someone with AK would have two pair (A,K). Someone with KQ has a straight draw, etc.\n\nGiven opponent’s earlier raise, they likely have:\n- Two pair (A,K; K,4; A,4)\n- Sets (unlikely, no paired board)\n- Draws (flush or straight)\n- Or overpairs like KK, AA (but AA beats you anyway)\n\nBut you have **A♦**, so only one Ace left (A♣), so AA is unlikely.\n\nYour options:\n- **Check back (0)**: See the river for free. Safe, but gives free card to draws.\n- **Bet small (e.g., 100)**: Charge draws, gain value from weaker hands.\n- **Bet larger**: Risk more, but may get folded out or trap yourself.\n- **All-in**: Overkill unless you’re committed.\n- **Fold**: Too strong a hand to fold — you have top pair.\n\nBest move: **Probe bet for value and protection**.\n\nSince opponent showed strength but checked the turn, a small **raise of 100** (to total bet of 100 this round) can serve well:\n- It denies free cards to draws (like QJ, QT, JT, or spade draws).\n- It extracts value from worse pairs (like A9, A8, A5, K9).\n- It may fold out weaker hands that might out-kick you.\n\nBut: opponent could have two pair (AK) or a set. Still, folding isn’t right.\n\nAlso, you’re behind in chips overall (-458 cumulative), so gaining pots is important.\n\nReason:  \nYou have top pair with a medium kicker. Opponent’s check on the turn after aggressive flop play suggests either weakness or a trap. Betting 100 is low-risk, high-reward: it protects against draws, gains value, and keeps the pot manageable. Going all-in or folding is too extreme.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
