es, the options are:\n  - Fold (-1)\n  - Call (match the raise amount — here 220 more)\n  - Raise (at least double previous raise → 440 minimum raise)\n\nBut here, **0 is listed as a valid action**, even after a raise. So in this system, **0 likely means "call"** when behind in betting.\n\nSo:\n- **0**: Call 220\n- **440+**: Raise (minimum 440)\n- **-2**: All-in\n\nSo your real choices are:\n- Call 220 (0): Stay in cheaply, see river.\n- Raise to at least 440: Build pot, potentially extract value or protect.\n- All-in: Overkill, but possible.\n\nGiven you have **quad 3s**, which is near-nut hand, you should **raise for value**. Your opponent may have:\n- A full house (e.g., 6-x making boat), like 6-7 or 6-K\n- Or weaker trips, two pair\nThey might call a raise with a full house or even top two pair.\n\nAlso, if they have a draw (flush or straight), raising protects and builds pot.\n\nRaising to 440 is good value — it’s the minimum raise, doesn’t scare too much, but grows pot.\n\nGoing all-in now (~19,800) is too aggressive — may cause fold from worse hands. Better to wait until river to push all-in if needed.\n\nBest move: **Raise to 440** — small but meaningful raise to build pot and gain information.\n\nReason:  \nYou have four of a kind (3s), a very strong hand. The only hand that beats you is 6-6 (quad 6s), which is statistically unlikely. Raising the bet increases the pot while likely getting called by inferior hands (like full houses with lower pairings or two pair). This maximizes expected value. Folding or checking loses value.\n\nAnswer: 440'}
